runs/b-bug-bounty.log — Bug bounty mechanics walkthrough (worked case)
Date: 2026-10-09 · Environment: local files only, no network, no platform accounts
Method: report drafted against the fictional Harborlight Books IDOR finding,
then self-reviewed against the five-part anatomy. No real submission (by design).

=== STEP 1 — Title (draft) ===
"IDOR in /invoice endpoint exposes other customers' invoices including billing details"
Check: mechanism (IDOR) + location (/invoice) + impact (other customers' billing details). Kept.

=== STEP 2 — Summary (draft) ===
"The invoice endpoint at GET /invoice?id=N performs no ownership check: any
authenticated user can read any customer's invoice by changing one digit in the
id parameter. Invoices contain the customer's full name, billing address, and
the last four digits of their payment card. The flaw was confirmed with two
test accounts owned by the reporter (alice, free tier; bob, free tier)."
Check: names the endpoint, the missing check, the worst honest consequence. OK.

=== STEP 3 — Reproduction steps (draft) ===
1. Create two free-tier accounts on harborlight-books.example (alice, bob).
   Both accounts are owned by the reporter; no other customer's data is used.
2. Log in as alice. GET /invoices — observe invoice IDs 1041 and 1042.
3. As alice, GET /invoice?id=1043.
4. Observe HTTP 200 with {"owner":"bob", ...} including bob's billing address
   and card last-four. This invoice is not in alice's list from step 2.
5. Log in as bob. GET /invoices — observe 1043 present: confirms the invoice
   belongs to another customer, not a rendering artifact.
Stranger test: every step names the exact request and the exact expected
observation; the account-ownership statement is explicit. PASS.

=== STEP 4 — Impact (draft) ===
"Every customer of the shop is affected. An attacker needs only a free account
and one changed digit to read any other customer's full name, billing address,
and card last-four. No privilege beyond the free tier is required. The flaw is
read-only: invoice creation, modification, and admin functions were tested and
are not reachable through this endpoint (401/403 as appropriate)."
Check: affected population, exact fields, prerequisites, and stated limits.
The restraint sentence stays — it is credibility. PASS.

=== STEP 5 — Remediation suggestion (draft) ===
"On every request to /invoice, compare the invoice's owner_id against the
authenticated user's id before serving; return 403 on mismatch (deny by
default). Apply the same check to every object endpoint that takes a
client-supplied identifier — the missing-check shape is the whole class."
One paragraph, fix class named. PASS.

=== STEP 6 — Self-review scores (0 missing / 1 weak / 2 triage-ready) ===
Title: 2 — mechanism, location, impact in one line.
Summary: 2 — flaw, location, worst honest consequence in three sentences.
Reproduction: 2 — numbered, stranger-testable, account ownership explicit.
Impact: 2 — population, fields, prerequisites, limits; no inflation.
Remediation: 2 — fix class in one paragraph.
Total: 10/10. One rewrite pass was considered for the title (first draft said
"invoice IDOR exposes customer data" — too vague; the kept version names the
endpoint and the exposed data). No further rewrites needed.

=== STEP 7 — Duplicate check (what would be checked before a real submission) ===
1. Same endpoint disclosed: search the program's Hacktivity for "/invoice" —
   an earlier identical disclosure means instant duplicate.
2. Same class across the app: check /order?id= and /receipt?id= endpoints —
   if the program already paid for the class, a second instance may still be
   valid (new endpoint) but must be framed as such.
3. Fixed in a recent release: re-test on the current build — a report against
   a patched endpoint is noise, not a finding.

=== STEP 8 — Scope re-read (fictional program policy) ===
Scope: harborlight-books.example web application only.
Out of scope: the payment processor (third party), the marketing blog,
any real customer's account (test accounts used are reporter-owned).
Confirmation: every step above touched only the in-scope web app, using
reporter-owned test accounts. No scanner was run; no third-party service
touched; no other person's data accessed. IN SCOPE — signed off in this log.

Walkthrough complete: 8/8 steps. Nothing submitted anywhere; nothing external touched.
